---
import "../styles/style.css";
import "../styles/about.css";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <title>fabbeyond.dev - About</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Quantico:wght@400;700&display=swap"
    />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Navbar />
    <div class="nav-bar-divider"></div>

    <main>
      <div class="about-content">
        <div class="about-intro-content">
          <p class="about-title">ABOUT</p>

          <p class="about-intro">
            My name is Fabian, or Fab for short, and I'm a 16-year-old
            programmer from Austria.
          </p>
          <p class="about-intro">
            Currently, while working on miscellaneous projects, I'm studying
            Game Design at{" "}
            <a href="https://www.spengergasse.at" target="_blank">
              HTL Spengergasse
            </a>{" "}
            in Vienna.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="about-tl-texts">
          <div class="about-texts">
            <p
              class="about-text"
              data-label="First Coding Experience"
              data-year="2018"
            >
              I had my first experience with coding around the age of 8. My dad
              introduced me to an app called{" "}
              <a href="https://www.scratchjr.org/">"Scratch Jr."</a> which is
              like Scratch but way more simple.
            </p>
            <p
              class="about-text"
              data-label="Scratch & Python"
              data-year="2018"
            >
              I quickly outgrew Scratch Jr and switched to the full version{" "}
              <a href="https://scratch.mit.edu/">"Scratch"</a>. This was my main
              programming language for a few years until I tried out Python. Due
              to my extensive use of Scratch I quickly grasped how written
              coding works and started making little projects like Quizzes,
              small drawings with the Turtle library and simple UIs with
              tkinter. Around that time I attended lots of coding summer camps
              for Scratch and Python. I was so advanced at that point that I
              even got to teach the other attending kids something.
            </p>

            <p class="about-text" data-label="Unity & C#" data-year="2022">
              Then I got really interested in Game Development and tried out
              Unity & C#, which I still use to make games. My first finished and
              released game,{" "}
              <a href="https://fabbeyond.itch.io/balanced-load">
                "Balanced Load"
              </a>
              , was made for the{" "}
              <a href="https://itch.io/jam/gmtk-2024">GMTK 2024 Game Jam</a>.
              Both other released games,{" "}
              <a href="https://fabbeyond.itch.io/fortuna">"Fortuna"</a> &{" "}
              <a href="https://fabbeyond.itch.io/ohcrab">"Oh, Crab!"</a>, were
              made for the internal Spengergasse Game Jams, 2025 & 2026
              respectively. "Fortuna" was made when I was in first year and
              actually won the Jam making me and my team the first ever
              first-year students to win the Game Jam.
            </p>
            <p class="about-text" data-label="Modding" data-year="2025">
              During the same time I also got interested in Modding. I started
              simple by making small mods for{" "}
              <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/2236070/Peaks_of_Yore/">
                "Peaks of Yore"
              </a>
              , a 3D physics-based climbing game. Once I got more comfortable
              modding I made slightly bigger and more complex mods for{" "}
              <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1030300/Hollow_Knight_Silksong/">
                "Silksong"
              </a>{" "}
              and{" "}
              <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/322170/Geometry_Dash/">
                "Geometry Dash"
              </a>
              . My experience with Unity helped tremendously with Modding Unity
              games and made it way easier to get started. Over the course of
              1.5 years all my mods reached around 60 thousand downloads, my
              most popular one{" "}
              <a href="https://geode-sdk.org/mods/fabbeyond.layouttools">
                "Layout Tools"
              </a>
              , for Geometry Dash, having amassed over 30k downloads.
            </p>

            <p class="about-text" data-label="Servers & Linux" data-year="2026">
              Around early 2026 I became interested in self hosting servers for
              various purposes like Discord bots, Minecraft servers, Websites,
              etc. I used my old PC as a home server which I run said things on.
              This means I have a decent understanding of Linux and servers as a
              whole.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="about-timeline">
            <div class="tl-line"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </main>

    <div class="nav-bar-divider"></div>
    <Footer />

    <script>
      const timeline = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".about-timeline");
      const texts = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".about-text");
      const line = timeline?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".tl-line");

      const createDots = () => {
        document
          .querySelectorAll(".tl-point, .tl-text")
          .forEach((i) => i.remove());

        if (timeline) {
          const dots = [...texts].map((i) => {
            const point = document.createElement("div");
            const dot = document.createElement("div");
            const text = document.createElement("p");
            const yearText = document.createElement("p");

            yearText.className = "tl-text tl-year";
            yearText.textContent = i.dataset.year;

            text.className = "tl-text";
            text.textContent = i.dataset.label;

            dot.className = "tl-dot";

            point.className = "tl-point";

            if (document.body.clientWidth <= 800) {
              i.before(text);
              i.before(yearText);
              point.append(dot);
            } else {
              point.append(yearText);
              point.append(dot);
              point.append(text);
            }
            timeline.append(point);

            return point;
          });

          const timelineTop = timeline.getBoundingClientRect().top;

          const centers = [...texts].map(
            (text) =>
              text.getBoundingClientRect().top -
              timelineTop +
              text.offsetHeight / 2,
          );
          centers.forEach((center, idx) => {
            dots[idx].style.top = `${center - dots[idx].offsetHeight / 2}px`;
          });
          if (line) {
            line.style.top = `${centers[0]}px`;
            line.style.height = `${centers[centers.length - 1] - centers[0]}px`;
          }
        }
      };
      createDots();
      new ResizeObserver(createDots).observe(document.body);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
